- * (C) 1994,95,96 by Torsten Scherer (TeSche)
- * itschere@techfak.uni-bielefeld.de
- *
- * - quite some changes for W1R1
- * - yet more changes for W1R2
+ * nxterm - the terminal window of the DSLinuxDSi desktop (rewritten for DSLinuxDSi; the old wterm-based nxterm only knew VT52 and threw
+ * away the ANSI sequences the shell's line editing and colours use).
  *
- * TeSche 01/96:
- * - supports W_ICON & W_CLOSE
- * - supports /etc/utmp logging for SunOS4 and Linux
- * - supports catching of console output for SunOS4
- * Phx 02-06/96:
- * - supports NetBSD-Amiga
- * Eero 11/97:
- * - unsetenv(DISPLAY), setenv(LINES, COLUMNS).
- * - Add new text modes (you need to use terminfo...).
- * Eero 2/98:
- * - Implemented fg/bgcolor setting.  With monochrome server it changes
- *   bgmode variable, which tells in which mode to draw to screen
- *   (M_CLEAR/M_DRAW) and affects F_REVERSE settings.
- * - Added a couple of checks.
+ *   nxterm [-g COLSxROWS] [-t title] [program args...]     default: a login shell, a window that fits the DS screen
  *
- * TODO:
- * - Allocate and set sensible window palette for fg/bg color setting.
- * - add scroll-region ('cs') command. Fairly many programs
- *   can take advantage of that.
- * - Add xterm like mouse event to terminfo key event conversion... :)
- * - check if current NetBSD really needs ifdefs below with
- *   current W server/library.
+ * A small VT100/ANSI terminal: cursor movement and positioning, erasing, inserting and deleting characters and lines, scrolling regions,
+ * 16 colours (bold = bright), reverse video, cursor on/off, autowrap with the usual deferred wrap at the last column, and answers to
+ * cursor position / device status requests. The screen is kept as cells, so the window redraws correctly when it is uncovered, and the last
+ * SBLINES lines that scrolled off the top can be looked at again with Page Up / Page Down (the X and Y buttons on the DSi); any other key
+ * returns to the live screen. TERM=vt100.
+ * Drawing: only rows that changed, as runs of equal colours (one request per run), and a whole-screen scroll is one copy request, because
+ * every request costs about a millisecond on the DS.
  */
